#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198016 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9d4e49c-202b-564f-839d-64b5d71a3783" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Are Attained on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/id #uuid "ed6483ae-971a-543b-adb6-72ac5809b602" :question/math-academy-id "e-12662" :question/problem "The maximum value of the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})= -x_1^2+2x_2^2+6x_3^2+4x_1x_2$, when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1$, is equal to $6$. Find a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ at which the maximum is attained." :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that:\n\n- The maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$, when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1$, is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n$\\max \\{Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: \\mid \\: \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}$\nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}$.\n- The minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$, when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1$, is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n$\\min \\{Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: \\mid \\: \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}$\nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{min}}$.\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}=6{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - 6I &= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix} - 6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of\n\n$$\n(A-6I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\ngives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{v}$ is already normalized (\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|\\mathbf{v}\\ &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n), we conclude that, $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198017 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6115493b-482c-5360-8d19-52582228e21d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Is Attained on the Unit Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "8a1adb2a-e9c5-52e1-bfa0-6977884fae42" :question/math-academy-id "e-12663" :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=9x_1^2-24x_1x_2+16x_2^2$, when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1$, is equal to $0$. Find a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ at which the minimum is attained." :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that:\n\n- The maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$, when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1$, is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n$\\max \\{Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: \\mid \\: \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}$\nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}$.\n- The minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$, when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1$, is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n$\\min \\{Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: \\mid \\: \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}$\nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{min}}$.\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}=0{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - 0I &= \\begin{bmatrix}9 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}9 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of\n\n$$\n(A-0I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\ngives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{v} \\| = 5\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198018 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17fd5c3d-d4c6-5f78-b262-fbc6b7209501" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Is Attained Over an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "a6abb2f6-6e59-59ac-8549-62d25b3b6e9b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12664" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})=16x_1^2+16x_1x_2+x_2^2$ given that $16x_1^2 + x_2^2=16$, and find the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ at which the minimum is obtained." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x_1^2}{1^2}+\\dfrac{x_2^2}{4^2}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=1$ (along the $x_1$-axis) and $b=4$ (along the $x_2$-axis).\n\nIf we introduce new variables\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\ny_1={x_1} \\quad\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\quad y_2 = \\dfrac{x_2}{4}\n$$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into\n\n$$\ny_1^2+y_2^2 = 1\n$$\n\nor\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|\\mathbf{y}\\ &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinates matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nwe obtain that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = P \\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = (P\\mathbf{y})^T A \\, (P\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{y}^T \\! (P^TAP) \\mathbf{y}= Q'(\\mathbf{y})\n$$\n\nAs a result, since $P^T=P$, the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nB &= P^{T}AP \\\\\n&= PAP \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}16 & 8 \\\\ 32 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}16 & 32 \\\\ 32 & 16\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we minimise $Q'(\\mathbf{y})$ when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\| \\mathbf{y} \\ &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas usual. Calculating the eigenvalues $B$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid B - λI \\mid &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}16 - λ & 32 \\\\ 32 & 16 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(16 - λ)(16 - λ) - 1024 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} - 32λ - 768 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 48)(λ + 16) &= 0 \\\\\nλ &= 48,-16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\min \\{Q'(\\mathbf{y}) \\: \\mid \\: \\| \\mathbf{y} \\ &= 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}} = -16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}=-16{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nB - (-16I) &= \\begin{bmatrix}16 & 32 \\\\ 32 & 16\\end{bmatrix} + 16\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}32 & 32 \\\\ 32 & 32\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of\n\n$$\n(B-(-16I))\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\ngives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{v} \\| = \\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nwe get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{min} &= Pu_{min} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6115493b-482c-5360-8d19-52582228e21d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Is Attained on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad6b9a7a-ab4f-57d1-b828-2aec3f786ad3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193818} {:answer/id #uuid "d89a4f8c-5ebf-518b-af6e-09a371c539f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193819} {:answer/id #uuid "d9350136-6e1f-5fa6-b8cb-4e7039b52e7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193820} {:answer/id #uuid "3714c497-f077-5a6d-884c-61e6e664e764" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193821} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd167c9-1ce7-5c30-a7ad-0c9743e01d3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bd167c9-1ce7-5c30-a7ad-0c9743e01d3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193822} :answer-field/id #uuid "3699d050-774a-5375-868c-6f50c0b89de2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193817}] :question/id #uuid "0419637b-40c5-5740-b2f6-97e085201516" :question/math-academy-id "q-136827" :question/problem "The maximum value of the quadratic form $Q(x) = x_{1}^{2} - 4x_{1}x_{2} + 4x_{2}^{2}$, when $‖x‖ = 1$, is equal to $5$. Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198488 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6115493b-482c-5360-8d19-52582228e21d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Is Attained on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd